    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **258 tests, 0 failures**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` derivation for owners \u2014 a read-only field derived from the city via the fixed table Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD, defaulting to `UNKNOWN`.\n\nChanges, following the existing conventions for derived owner fields (`displayName`, `membershipTier`, etc.):\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the OpenAPI generator produces `OwnerDto.locality` for the `GET /api/owners/{id}` response.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerLocality.java`** (new) \u2014 a small helper holding the fixed city-to-region table with `forCity(city)` returning the canonical region or `\"UNKNOWN\"` (null-safe).\n\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 a `@Mapping` expression on `toOwnerDto` that sets `locality` from `owner.getCity()` via `OwnerLocality.forCity(...)`.\n\nA note on why the helper is a separate class rather than a method on the mapper: my first attempt put the lookup as a `static String localityFor(String)` inside the `OwnerMapper` interface, which caused MapStruct to treat it as a generic `String\u2192String` conversion and apply it to *every* string field (firstName, address, \u2026). Extracting it into a plain, non-mapper class fixed that \u2014 MapStruct only invokes it through the explicit expression.",
